  "output": "Based on the code shown, here's a focused performance analysis with concrete fixes.\n\n## 1. N+1 query patterns\nNot visible in this frontend slice (that's a backend/Mongo concern — already flagged in prior sessions re: `analytics` endpoints doing in-memory aggregation instead of Mongo pipelines). Nothing new to add here for the frontend files shown.\n\n## 2. Unnecessary React re-renders\n\n**`useToasts.ts`** — `showToast`'s `setTimeout` isn't cleared on unmount, and every toast schedules an independent timer. Low risk but worth noting alongside the memory-leak point below.\n\n**`useKeyboardShortcuts.ts`** is actually already well-optimized (refs avoid re-registration) — no action needed.\n\nThe bigger known offender (per prior audit, not in this slice) is `CampaignsView`'s `dialing_strategy` object being rebuilt every render. Fix pattern:\n\n```tsx\nconst strategy = useMemo(() => buildDialingStrategy(campaign), [campaign._id, campaign.dialing_strategy]);\n```\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities\n\n**`src/utils/cache.ts`** — solid localStorage cache utility, but nothing in the shown files actually calls `cacheGet`/`cacheSet` for API reads (e.g. `OUTCOME_CATEGORIES`, `phrases`, `outcomes` from `api.ts` look like good candidates — they're near-static reference data fetched repeatedly).\n\n```ts\n// api.ts\nexport async function outcomes(): Promise<OutcomeRule[]> {\n  const cached = cacheGet<OutcomeRule[] | null>('outcomes', null);\n  if (cached) return cached;\n  const data = await request<OutcomeRule[]>('/outcomes');\n  cacheSet('outcomes', data);\n  return data;\n}\n```\n\n**No HTTP cache headers** — `request()` in `api.ts` never sets `Cache-Control`/uses `fetch`'s cache option for GET requests. For read-heavy, rarely-changing endpoints (phrases, outcomes, language settings), consider `cache: 'force-cache'` with a short-lived ETag check, or a simple in-memory TTL cache in front of `request()`.\n\n## 4. Memory leaks\n\n**`useCountUp.ts`** — `requestAnimationFrame(tick)` is never cancelled on unmount or when `target`/`duration` changes mid-animation. If the component unmounts while animating, `tick` keeps calling `setState` on an unmounted component and touching a stale `ref.current`.\n\n```ts\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (target === 0) { setValue(0); return; }\n  let rafId: number;\n  let cancelled = false;\n  const start = performance.now();\n  ref.current?.classList.add('counting');\n  const tick = (now: number) => {\n    if (cancelled) return;\n    const progress = Math.min((now - start) / duration, 1);\n    const eased = 1 - Math.pow(1 - progress, 3);\n    setValue(Math.round(target * eased));\n    if (progress < 1) rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n    else ref.current?.classList.remove('counting');\n  };\n  rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n  return () => { cancelled = true; cancelAnimationFrame(rafId); };\n}, [target, duration]);\n```\n\n**`useToasts.ts`** — `setTimeout(() => dismissToast(id), durationMs)` isn't tracked/cleared. If the component using this hook unmounts before the timeout fires, `dismissToast` still calls `setState` on an unmounted component (React 18 tolerates this silently but it's a leak of the timer + closure until it fires). Track timer IDs and clear on an unmount effect:\n\n```ts\nconst timersRef = useRef<Set<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>>>(new Set());\nuseEffect(() => () => timersRef.current.forEach(clearTimeout), []);\n// in showToast: const t = setTimeout(...); timersRef.current.add(t);\n```\n\n## 5. Redundant computations\n\n**`utils/transcript.ts`** `buildConversationItems` — recomputes `events.filter(...)` and does two full-array filter/sort passes on every call. If this is invoked per-render in a view with a large transcript list, wrap the call site in `useMemo` keyed on `transcript._id` + `events.length` rather than optimizing the function itself (the function is pure and fine; the caller is where re-renders would cause redundant work).\n\n**`utils/formatting.ts`** `parseApiDate` + regex test runs on every `formatDate`/`formatTime`/`timeAgo` call — fine for isolated calls, but if used inside a list render (e.g., transcripts table with hundreds of rows re-rendering per poll), consider memoizing formatted values per row via `useMemo` in the row component rather than reformatting on every parent re-render.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line — 3 concrete action items:**\n1. Fix the `requestAnimationFrame` leak in `useCountUp.ts` (real bug, unmount-safe fix shown above).\n2. Track/clear `setTimeout` IDs in `useToasts.ts`.\n3. Add a cache layer (`cacheGet`/`cacheSet`, already built in `utils/cache.ts` but unused) in front of static reference-data API calls (`outcomes`, `phrases`, `languageSettings`) in `api.ts`.\n\nThese are the only concrete, verifiable issues in the files provided — I don't have visibility into `App.tsx`, `CampaignsView`, or `TranscriptsView` in this context (only referenced from prior memory), so I can't verify those specific claims against current code. Want me to pull those files and confirm/fix the re-render issues directly?\n",
